<Folder>
<name>Touchdowns</name>
<Placemark><name>TD1</name><description><![CDATA[Sink rate 103 fpm, peak load 1.13 g]]></description><styleUrl>#touchdown</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.009166,38.522035,5.8</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder>
<name>Events</name>
<Placemark><name>Excessive bank from 1000 ft to 500 ft</name><description><![CDATA[Medium · T+230 s<br/>42° bank, limit 30°<br/>Bank angle from 1000 ft to 500 ft, held 3 s. EASA Data4Safety, above 500 ft: more than 15 deg (SL1) or 30 deg (SL2A).<br/>Why it matters: Still turning steeply on final means the aircraft is not lined up, and bank raises the stall speed close to the ground.<br/>What to do: Plan the turn onto final so it is complete with shallow bank well before 500 ft.]]></description><styleUrl>#medium</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.011137,38.544621,310.2</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>High descent rate above 500 ft</name><description><![CDATA[Medium · T+230 s<br/>2990 fpm sink, limit 1500 fpm<br/>Descent rate from 1000 ft to 500 ft. EASA Data4Safety, above 500 ft: more than 1200 fpm (SL1) or 1500 fpm (SL2A). Held 5 s, Data4Safety's persistence for the first threshold (3 s for the second; a rule has one).<br/>Why it matters: A sustained high descent rate close to the ground leaves little room to arrest it and is a classic precursor of hard landings and controlled flight into terrain.<br/>What to do: Stay on a stable path; if it needs more than 1000 fpm below 1000 ft, the approach is too high: go around and set up again.]]></description><styleUrl>#medium</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.011141,38.541228,228.0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Excessive bank below 500 ft</name><description><![CDATA[Medium · T+242 s<br/>27° bank, limit 15°<br/>Bank angle below 500 ft, held 3 s. EASA Data4Safety, below 500 ft: more than 15 deg (SL2B) or 30 deg (SL3). Light aircraft: `bank-light`.<br/>Why it matters: Steep bank close to the ground raises the stall speed (about 7% at 20 deg, 41% at 45 deg) and risks a wingtip strike; a stall in a low turn is rarely recoverable.<br/>What to do: Plan the pattern so the turn to final is complete with shallow bank (15 deg or less) well above 500 ft. Overshooting final: go around, do not tighten the turn.]]></description><styleUrl>#medium</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.008640,38.538133,141.8</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>High descent rate at 500 ft</name><description><![CDATA[Advisory · T+242 s<br/>2062 fpm sink, limit 1000 fpm<br/>Rate of descent at the 500 ft gate against the FSF ALAR stabilised approach limit, 1000 fpm. An advisory: the descent below 500 ft is graded by `high-sink-below-500` and `high-sink-light`.<br/>Why it matters: A steep, fast descent at 500 ft leaves little height to arrest the sink, a common precursor of hard and short landings.<br/>What to do: Fly a stable path of about 3 degrees (roughly 500-700 fpm in a light aircraft, 700-800 fpm in a jet). More than 1000 fpm at the gate: go around.]]></description><styleUrl>#advisory</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.009253,38.538972,157.8</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bank at 500 ft</name><description><![CDATA[Advisory · T+242 s<br/>15° bank, limit 7°<br/>Bank angle at the 500 ft gate against the EAFDM stabilised-approach limit, 7 deg. An advisory: bank is graded by `bank-above-500`, `bank-below-500` and `bank-light`.<br/>Why it matters: Still turning at 500 ft means the aircraft is not lined up with the runway, and bank raises the stall speed close to the ground.<br/>What to do: Roll out on final above 500 ft: extend the downwind or widen the base turn so the final is straight and wings-level. Not aligned at the gate: go around.]]></description><styleUrl>#advisory</styleUrl><Point><altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode><coordinates>27.009253,38.538972,157.8</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
